ifacts and sub-second verification.\nL8: \nL9: 【0†Get in touch】\nL10: \nL11: Table of contents\nL12: \nL13: Table of Contents\nL14: \nL15: Related Resource\nL16: \nL17: 【2†[ Compliance ]Pacing AI Requires Proof】\nL18: \nL19: 【3†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com】\nL20: \nL21: AI workloads are easy to claim and hard to prove.\nL22: A model provider can say that a particular model processed a particular input. A cloud can say it served the requested weights and architecturea. An AI lab can say its system followed an approved policy. But unless the relying party can inspect and rerun the entire computation, those claims ultimately depend on trust.\nL23: Zero-knowledge proofs can replace that trust with mathematics. A proof can establish that a committed model produced a specific output from a specific input, without revealing the model’s weights or other private information.\nL24: \nL25: The problem has always been performance.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2002.07648] Compact Merkle Multiproofs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.07648)\nciteturn13view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.07648\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 170\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Cryptography and Security\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 18 Feb 2020 (【5†v1】), last revised 24 Feb 2020 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Compact Merkle Multiproofs\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Lum Ramabaja】, 【7†Arber Avdullahu】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Compact Merkle Multiproofs, by Lum Ramabaja and 1 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:The compact Merkle multiproof is a new and significantly more memory-efficient way to generate and verify sparse Merkle multiproofs. A standard sparse Merkle multiproof requires to store an index for every non-leaf hash in the multiproof. The compact Merkle multiproof on the other hand requires only $k$ leaf indices, where $k$ is the number of elements used for creating a multiproof. This significantly reduces the size of multirpoofs, especially for larger Merke trees.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMultiproof optimisations by ajhavlin · Pull Request #173 · arkworks-rs/crypto-primitives · GitHub (https://github.com/arkworks-rs/crypto-primitives/pull/173)\nciteturn13view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/arkworks-rs/crypto-primitives/pull/173\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 615\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: \nL5: Appearance settings\nL6: \nL7:   * Platform\nL8: \nL9:     * AI CODE CREATION\nL10:       * 【2†GitHub CopilotWrite better code with AI】\nL11:       * 【3†GitHub Copilot appDirect agents from issue to merge】\nL12:       * 【4†MCP RegistryIntegrate external tools】\nL13: \nL14:     * DEVELOPER WORKFLOWS\nL15:       * 【5†ActionsAutomate any workflow】\nL16:       * 【6†CodespacesInstant dev environments】\nL17:       * 【7†IssuesPlan and track work】\nL18:       * 【8†Code ReviewManage code changes】\nL19:       * 【9†Code QualityEnforce quality at merge】\nL20:     * APPLICATION SECURITY\nL21:       * 【10†GitHub Advanced SecurityFind and fix vulnerabilities】\nL22:       * 【11†Code securitySecure your code as you build】\nL23:       * 【12†Secret protectionStop leaks before they start】\nL24: \nL25:     * EXPLORE\nL26:       * 【13†Why GitHub】\nL27:       * 【14†Documentation†docs.github.com】\nL28:       * 【15†Blog†github.blog】\nL29:       * 【16†Changelog†github.blog】\nL30:       * 【17†Marketplace】\nL31: \nL32: 【18†View all features】\nL33: \nL34:   * Solutions\nL35:     * BY COMPANY SIZE\nL36:       * 【19†Enterprises】\nL37:       * 【20†Small and medium teams】\nL38:       * 【21†Startups】\nL39:       * 【22†Nonprofits】\nL40: \nL41:     * BY USE CASE\nL42:       * 【23†App Modernization】\nL43:       * 【24†DevSecOps】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.14849] StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14849)\nciteturn13view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14849\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 166\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Cryptography and Security\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 10 Oct 2026 (【5†v1】), last revised 26 Oct 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Jinwook Kim】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine, by Jinwook Kim\nL14: \nL15: 【7†View PDF】 【8†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Authenticated state transitions need proof engines that conserve memory, avoid repeated setup, keep every result bound to its request under parallel execution and return complete proofs at service rates. We present StateSync-GKR, a reusable Rust sumcheck/GKR core with a sparse-Merkle frontend, and evaluate release 1.2.0 against release 1.1 measured on the same day on the same instance types, request corpus and latency budgets.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.37710] zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37710)\nciteturn13view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37710\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Hardware Architecture\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 26 Oct 2026]\nL9: # Title:zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Samarth Narang】, 【6†Alhad Daftardar】, 【7†Brandon Reagen】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models, by Samarth Narang and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:SumCheck is a central prover primitive in zero-knowledge proofs, exposing table-parallel work that maps naturally to GPUs but becomes increasingly arithmetic-intensive as finite fields widen. We study how this shift affects the cost of programming SumCheck in Triton rather than CUDA.\nL17: Our benchmark, zkDuel, provides matched implementations of 25 zkPHIRE-derived constraints alongside diagnostic polynomials, covering 32-, 64-, 128-, and 256-bit prime fields, with independent CPU-reference validation and cross-backend checksum gating. Of 800 configurations attempted on an NVIDIA H100, 758 complete with matching checksums.\n"
